  <article class="ai-model">
    <p class="ai-model-kicker">Frontier models</p>
    <h1 class="ai-model-title">Gemini 4 Carbon</h1>
    <p class="ai-model-dek">Gemini 4 Carbon is a Google DeepMind model announced 28 October 2026. In <a href="https://blog.google/innovation-and-ai/models-and-research/gemini-models/gemini-4-carbon-now-available/">Gemini 4 Carbon is now available</a>, dated 10 November 2026, Google says it is “now available” and is “making Gemini 4 Carbon available to developers, enterprises, scientists, and consumers” [1]. Google says it is “available via the API in Google AI Studio and Gemini for business for all paying developers and enterprises,” and that it is “rolling out to all Google AI Ultra subscribers in Google Antigravity, the Gemini app, and AI Mode in Search” [1]. Everyday Ultra users can “Try Carbon starting today” in the Gemini app and AI Mode in Search [1]. <a href="https://aistudio.google.com/docs/whats-new-gemini-4-carbon">What’s new in Gemini 4 Carbon</a> says it is “available on the paid tier of the Gemini API and in Google AI Studio” [2]. It is not available on the Free tier, per the <a href="https://ai.google.dev/gemini-api/docs/pricing">Gemini Developer API pricing</a> page [2][3]. Google says cybersecurity defenders can “Apply to join the Fairwind Program to get access to Carbon for cyber on a rolling basis” [1]. Google says <a href="/ai/models/google/gemini-4-argon/">Gemini 4 Argon</a> “remains the ideal choice for the majority of software engineering and knowledge work,” while Carbon is built for tasks that test the limits of a model’s intelligence [1]. What’s new says they share the same context window, built-in tools, and per-token pricing, and that the main difference is how many thinking tokens each model spends per turn [2]. It does not say Carbon replaces Argon [1][2]. The API id, the name a program uses to call the model, is <code>gemini-4-carbon</code>, a Stable version string, per the <a href="https://aistudio.google.com/docs/models/gemini-4-carbon">Gemini 4 Carbon model page</a> [4]. Context is a unified 1,048,576-token window for input and output. Max output is 65,536 tokens by default, or up to 1,048,576 with long decode [2][4]. The Standard price is $2 per million input tokens and $10 per million output tokens through 30 November 2026, then $4 and $20 starting 1 December 2026, the same Standard schedule as Argon [1][2][3]. Tokens are the word pieces models are billed by. Output price includes thinking tokens [3].</p>
    <hr class="ai-model-rule" />
    <ModelFacts />
    <h2 class="ai-model-section">Training</h2>
    <p>In <a href="https://blog.google/innovation-and-ai/models-and-research/gemini-models/introducing-gemini-4-carbon/">Gemini 4 Carbon: our next frontier model</a> (28 October 2026), Google says it trained Carbon to handle complex or high-pressure tasks the right way, and cut shortcut attempts during training [5]. Google says Carbon brought successful cheating on Terminal-Bench 4.0 to 0.0%, with cheating attempts at 0.4%, while its overall score went up. Those are Google’s figures [5]. Parameter count, training compute, dataset, and knowledge cutoff are not in the sources on this page.</p>
    <h2 class="ai-model-section">Inference</h2>
    <p>Google says Carbon is built for long agent work, and that its own teams have seen it run for days on multi-step engineering jobs, such as finding and patching subtle bugs across large codebases [5]. Thinking is supported at <code>low</code>, <code>medium</code>, and <code>high</code>. The default thinking level is <code>medium</code>. Thinking tokens are billed as output [2][3][4]. Input, thinking, and output tokens share the 1,048,576-token window [2]. The Carbon model page lists inputs as text, image, video, audio, and PDF, and output as text [4]. <a href="https://artificialanalysis.ai/models/gemini-4-carbon">Artificial Analysis</a> lists text, image, video, and speech input, with text output. That list is Artificial Analysis’s, not a Google sentence [6]. Tokens per second, rate limits, and regions are not in the sources on this page.</p>
    <h2 class="ai-model-section">Context</h2>
    <p>Context is how much text the model can take in and write in one job. The Carbon model page lists a unified input and output token limit of 1,048,576 tokens [4]. Max output is 65,536 tokens for a default synchronous response, or up to 1,048,576 with long decode [2][4]. What’s new says a single response can go past 64K tokens, up to 1 million tokens minus the input [2]. Artificial Analysis also lists a 1 million token context window [6].</p>
    <h2 class="ai-model-section">Methods</h2>
    <p>Google says Carbon uses the same layered safety setup as Argon: model training, real-time misuse defenses, prompt-injection resistance, and monitoring of the chain of thought [5]. Google says it is taking part in the U.S. government’s voluntary process for testing models before release [5]. In live coding with its own teams, Google says high-severity misalignment alerts fell by almost 80%, and in internal tests Carbon made about half as many unsupported claims as Argon [5]. Google says Carbon had 0.0% attack success on Gray Swan’s Indirect Prompt Injection benchmark, against 0.7% for Argon [5]. Indirect prompt injection means hidden instructions in a webpage, email, or file that try to hijack the model. Those are Google’s figures [5].</p>
    <h2 class="ai-model-section">Benchmarking</h2>
    <p>Artificial Analysis scores Gemini 4 Carbon (high) at 60 on its Intelligence Index v5.0.1, fetched 9 November 2026 (59 on v5.0, fetched 28 October 2026), per the <a href="https://artificialanalysis.ai/leaderboards/models">Artificial Analysis LLM leaderboard</a> and the <a href="https://artificialanalysis.ai/articles/gemini-4-carbon-new-number-1">Artificial Analysis Carbon article</a> [7][8]. The article reports the results for Gemini 4 Carbon with high reasoning [8]. Counting each model once, that is the second-highest model after Claude Fable 5.2 (max) at 65, and one point above Claude Opus 5.5 (max) at 59. Gemini 4 Argon (high) scores 50 on the same index [7]. The <a href="https://artificialanalysis.ai/changelog">Artificial Analysis changelog</a> records Intelligence Index v5.0.1 on 3 November 2026 and says it incorporated grading robustness improvements from the CritPt authors and regraded all models on CritPt v2 [9]. The same changelog records Intelligence Index v5.0 on 27 October 2026 [9]. That Index score is an independent score, not a Google chart number.</p>
    <p>Artificial Analysis said in its 28 October write-up that Carbon had the top score on 4 of the 9 Index tests then, and that it was not leading in coding and knowledge work [8]. On the Artificial Analysis Coding Agent Index, Carbon (high) in Antigravity CLI scores 59, behind Claude Code with Claude Opus 5.5 (max) at 62 [8].</p>
    <p>On Terminal-Bench 4.0, Artificial Analysis scores Gemini 4 Carbon (high) second at <strong>64%</strong>. On FrontierCode v1.1, Carbon scores <strong>51%</strong>, behind Opus 5.5 (max) at 56%. On AA-Omniscience v1.1, Carbon scores <strong>54</strong>, six points ahead of Claude Opus 5.5 (max) at 48 and eight points ahead of Gemini 4 Argon at 46 [8]. Those are independent scores, not Google chart numbers.</p>
    <p>In the 10 November post, Google says Carbon “ranks #1 on CursorBench 4.0, Perplexity’s WANDR, and Zapier’s AutomationBench at more than 2X lower cost per task compared to the next best external model” [1]. Those figures were not independently re-checked here. They are Google’s claims. This site’s #aiBench CursorBench and AutomationBench cells for Carbon stay blank.</p>
    <p>The first three cells below (SEC-bench Pro, ExploitGym, and CWE-bench) are lab claims from Google’s 28 October post. Google says those cyber evals were “evaluated without guardrails” [5]. WebDev Arena is a separate lab claim from the same post; Google does not attach the “without guardrails” note to it [5].</p>
